.Dd $Mdocdate: August 2 2026 $
.Dt Fugu::File 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm Fugu::File
.Nd file operations for a daemon and its tools
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
use Fugu::File;

my $data = Fugu::File->read('/etc/mydaemon.conf');

Fugu::File->write_atomic('/var/db/myapp/key', $key, mode => 0600);
Fugu::File->write_json('/var/db/myapp/state.json', $state,
    mode => 0600);

Fugu::File->ensure_dir('/var/db/myapp');
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
holds the file operations that a daemon and its tools repeat.
Every method is a class method over a path.
The module keeps no state.
.Pp
A recoverable failure returns
.Dv undef
and reports through
.Xr Fugu::Log 3p .
A class-method module has no object to hold an error accessor, so the
log is where the reason goes.
.Pp
The write methods set the mode at the open, before the first byte.
A
.Xr chmod 2
after the write leaves a window in which a secret is world-readable,
and that window is the whole point of the mode.
.Ss read
.Fn read "$path"
returns the whole content of the file, as bytes.
The method returns
.Dv undef
when the file does not open.
An empty file reads as the empty string, which is not the same answer.
.Ss write
.Fn write "$path" "$data" "%args"
writes the bytes to the file.
An existing file goes away first, so the mode argument means what it
says instead of yielding to the mode the old file had.
.Pp
This is the argument:
.Bl -tag -width "modeXX"
.It Fa mode
The file mode.
The default is 0644.
.El
.Ss write_atomic
.Fn write_atomic "$path" "$data" "%args"
writes through a temporary file in the same directory, then renames
over the target.
A reader sees the old content or the new content, and never a
half-written file.
The argument is the same as for
.Fn write .
.Pp
The temporary file is a sibling of the target, because
.Xr rename 2
is atomic only inside one filesystem.
.Ss read_json
.Fn read_json "$path"
reads and decodes a JSON file.
The method returns
.Dv undef
when the file is absent, empty, or not valid JSON.
A state file that a crash truncated is a recoverable condition: the
caller starts over.
.Ss write_json
.Fn write_json "$path" "$ref" "%args"
encodes and writes a JSON file with
.Fn write_atomic .
The argument is the same.
.Pp
The encoding is canonical, so the same data always gives the same
bytes.
Thus a difference between two state files is a difference in the
state.
.Ss ensure_dir
.Fn ensure_dir "$path" "%args"
makes sure the directory exists.
.Pp
The method refuses a symlink and refuses a path that exists as
something else.
Both are conditions that a daemon must not write through: a symlink
puts the daemon's files where the owner of the link decided.
.Pp
This is the argument:
.Bl -tag -width "modeXX"
.It Fa mode
The mode for directories that the method creates.
The default is 0755.
.El
.Ss expand_tilde
.Fn expand_tilde "$path"
replaces a leading tilde with the home directory.
The method leaves every other path unchanged, and leaves
.Pa ~user
alone: this module does not resolve other users.
.Ss share_path
.Fn share_path "$relative" "%args"
resolves a file that ships with the program.
The search starts beside the module tree, so a source checkout finds
its own copy, and falls back to the working directory.
The method returns the first path that exists, or
.Dv undef .
.Pp
This is the argument:
.Bl -tag -width "rootXX"
.It Fa root
A directory to search before the others.
.El
.Ss atomic_dir
.Fn atomic_dir "$target" "$code"
builds a directory tree beside
.Fa $target
and publishes it with one
.Xr rename 2 .
.Fa $code
receives the temporary directory and returns true when the tree is
complete.
A false return, a die, or a signal discards the tree.
.Pp
The method refuses to overwrite an existing target.
A rename over a populated directory is not atomic, so a caller that
wants a replacement removes the old tree itself.
.Pp
A build can run for minutes over a large file.
The method therefore installs handlers for
.Dv SIGINT
and
.Dv SIGTERM
for the length of the call, and an
.Ic END
block covers the rest.
.Ss sweep_temp
.Fn sweep_temp "$parent"
removes the leftover build directories under
.Fa $parent .
A kill that no handler can catch leaves them.
The method returns the count it removed.
.Ss valid_name
.Fn valid_name "$name"
reports if the string is safe as one path component.
.Pp
The check refuses an empty name, a name longer than 255 characters, a
name that holds a path separator or a NUL byte, and the two directory
entries
.Pa \&.
and
.Pa \&.. .
Every one of them turns a name that came from a caller into a path
that reaches outside the directory it was meant for.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn read
returns the content of the file.
.Fn read_json
returns the decoded structure.
Both return
.Dv undef
on failure.
.Pp
.Fn write ,
.Fn write_atomic ,
.Fn write_json
and
.Fn ensure_dir
return 1 on success and
.Dv undef
on failure.
.Pp
.Fn atomic_dir
returns
.Fa $target
on success and
.Dv undef
otherwise.
.Fn sweep_temp
returns a count.
.Fn valid_name
returns 1 or 0.
.Fn expand_tilde
and
.Fn share_path
return a path.
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example stores a key that only its owner may read:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
Fugu::File->ensure_dir('/var/db/myapp', mode => 0700)
    or die "cannot prepare the state directory\en";

Fugu::File->write_atomic('/var/db/myapp/ltsk', $secret,
    mode => 0600)
    or die "cannot store the key\en";
.Ed
.Pp
This example builds a cache entry that is never half-published:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
Fugu::File->atomic_dir("$cache/$key", sub ($tmp) {
    convert($disk, "$tmp/base") or return 0;
    Fugu::File->write_json("$tmp/meta.json", $meta, mode => 0600)
        or return 0;
    return 1;
});
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
No method dies.
Each one reports a failure through its return value and a message in
the log.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chmod 2 ,
.Xr rename 2 ,
.Xr Fugu::Log 3p ,
.Xr Fugu::Store 3p ,
.Xr JSON::PP 3p
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
.Fn read
holds the whole file in memory.
Do not use it for a file without a bound.
.Pp
.Fn write_atomic
is atomic against a reader, not against another writer.
Two processes that write the same path at the same time both succeed,
and the last rename wins.
Use
.Xr Fugu::Pidfile 3p
or a lock of your own where that matters.
